<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Operation Trikora</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1961">1961</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tri Komando Rakyat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Operation Trikora was the Indonesian campaign of 1961–1962 to take western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, named after the 'People's Triple Command' that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> proclaimed in December 1961. Indonesian forces, under a command led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>, landed paratroops and infiltrators while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> lost a naval clash in the Arafura Sea. Under American pressure the Netherlands signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Agreement of August 1962, and after a brief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> administration the territory passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in 1963.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/operation-trikora.jpg" alt="Operation Trikora" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lou Sander · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yos_Sudarso_Postage_Stamp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> became independent as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in December 1949, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, its transfer to be discussed over the next 12 months, and then sought to retain it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and some West Papuan leaders argued that the West Papuans were ethnically and geographically separate and had always been administered apart, while Indonesian nationalists regarded the territory, Irian Barat, as part of their state. Votes at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> reached a majority but not the two-thirds needed. From 1954 Indonesia made sporadic raids, nationalised Dutch businesses, repatriated Dutch nationals and in August 1960 severed ties with the Netherlands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In December 1960 General Nasution negotiated an arms package of US$2.5 billion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, which brought Indonesia MiG fighters, Tu-16 bombers, 12 Whiskey-class submarines and a Sverdlov-class cruiser renamed the KRI Irian. In his speech at Yogyakarta on 19 December 1961 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> set out the three principles of Trikora: to prevent a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> puppet state in West Papua, to fly the Indonesian flag there and to prepare mass mobilisation. In 1962 Indonesia inserted 562 troops by sea and 1,154 by air drop, who fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> but were largely militarily ineffective; at least 94 Indonesian soldiers were killed. Khrushchev later admitted that Soviet personnel had commanded Indonesian submarines and piloted Tu-16s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By August 1962 a force of 7,000 paratroopers, 4,500 marines and 13,000 army servicemen was preparing an assault on Biak, the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> base, as Operation Jayawijaya. Unwilling to be drawn into a protracted conflict, and pressed by the Kennedy administration, which wanted to court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> away from the Soviet orbit, the Dutch signed the agreement negotiated by the US diplomat Ellsworth Bunker, and the assault was called off. The territory passed on 1 October 1962 to the United Nations Temporary Executive Authority, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> formally annexed it on 1 May 1963.</p><p>The agreement provided for a plebiscite, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-free-choice">Act of Free Choice</a>, held in 1969, after which West Irian was formally integrated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Journalists and diplomats criticised the vote as rigged; 84 member states voted for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> to accept the result, with 30 abstaining. Many West Papuans refused to accept integration and formed the Free Papua Movement (Organisasi Papua Merdeka), which waged an insurgency against the Indonesian authorities. Monuments to the operation stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, Makassar and other cities, and Trikora Peak in the Papua highlands is named after it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Operation Trikora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sukarno used the military to press claims abroad: <b>Operation Trikora</b> of 1961–1962 forced the Dutch out of western New Guinea, which passed to Indonesia in 1963 after a brief United Nations administration, and the Confrontation of 1963–1966 waged an undeclared border war against the new state of Malaysia…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
